# Research and engineering POC: retrieval, deduplication and grouping of theses by embeddings and reranking

You are reviewing read-only. Write one report in English (Markdown) to the output file. Do not edit the repository, do not call any model API, do not spend money. Think deeply; this is a research and engineering plan, not a summary.

## Why

Hivemind (this repository) builds a library of theses (dated questions with ladders) from financial documents. Today every step that needs to find "the same thing" or "related things" asks an LLM over free text, with a trigram full-text search as the only candidate recall: matching a new candidate thesis against existing theses (`src/hivemind/views/matching.py`, `store.search_views`), evidence repeat/replace decisions (`rank_evidence`), research-group membership (`src/hivemind/views/groups.py`, D149/D165/D166) and the idea route's view lookup (`src/hivemind/search.py`, `src/hivemind/idea.py`, which already uses OpenAI embeddings over each thesis's text). The owner (Alex) wants to know whether embedding-based recall, followed by reranking (a cross-encoder, an LLM reranker, or key-based rules), can do lookup, deduplication and grouping accurately and cheaply, as the base for the next engineering stage. His standing note: the pipeline is deliberately brute-force LLM prototyping; it must work and replicate stably before cost optimisation, so this POC must be measured against the current pipeline's results, not replace it blindly.

